        body:
          'Every ingredient of a revenge play is in place by the end of Act One: a murdered father, a named killer, a son with a sword. What Shakespeare withholds is the swing. Hamlet learns his target within hours and needs four more acts to strike, and the whole tragedy grows inside that gap. Had he killed Claudius on the battlements, Denmark would have paid one life instead of eight.\n\nThe play refuses to settle on a single reason for the delay, and that refusal is its genius. Hamlet accuses himself of cowardice in one soliloquy, doubts the ghost’s honesty in another, and reasons himself out of an easy kill when he finds Claudius at prayer, arguing that a soul dispatched mid-confession would rise to heaven rather than fall. Each explanation convinces on its own and none of them is sufficient. Watching Fortinbras march twenty thousand men toward a worthless patch of Poland, he marvels at people who can act on far less cause than he has.\n\nWhen action finally comes it arrives sideways, in the stab through the arras and the improvised bloodbath of the duel, never as the deliberate deed the ghost requested. Hamlet’s late acceptance that "the readiness is all" registers less as triumph than as exhaustion, the surrender of a man who has stopped waiting for a perfect moment. Shakespeare thereby invented a hero whose inner life is more gripping than his plot, and the hesitation that ruins the revenge is exactly what makes the character immortal.',
