        body:
          'Macbeth is Shakespeare’s anatomy of ambition cut loose from conscience, and it is remarkable chiefly for how much conscience it grants the murderer. Before Duncan dies, Macbeth lists every reason not to kill him: the king is his kinsman, his guest, and a gentle ruler whose virtues will plead like angels against the deed. On the other side of the ledger he can find nothing at all except "vaulting ambition, which o’erleaps itself," and he knows that the word is an accusation rather than a defense. A duller man would rationalize. Macbeth sees perfectly clearly and proceeds anyway.\n\nThe play then demonstrates that ambition of this kind cannot stop once it reaches its object. Duncan’s death buys a crown that instantly requires defending, so Banquo must die because the witches promised his descendants the future, and Macduff’s wife and children must die because Macduff has slipped away to England. Each crime is smaller in motive and larger in cruelty than the one before it, until Macbeth is butchering a household that poses him no threat. He names the mechanism himself when he admits that he is so far steeped in blood that turning back would be as tedious as going on.\n\nWhat makes the descent tragic rather than merely criminal is how knowingly he forfeits everything. He predicted the outcome in advance, imagining that he was trading his eternal jewel for a barren scepter, and by the final act the prediction has come true in detail. Honor, obedience, troops of friends: he must not look to have any of them, only mouth-honor from men who curse him under their breath. The crown is genuine and worthless. Shakespeare’s warning is not that ambition fails, but that it can succeed completely and still leave nothing behind.',
