        body:
          'The most modern element of Macbeth is not the murder but the aftermath. Once he holds the throne he cannot enjoy it, because power taken by force has to be held by force, and every ally becomes a potential witness. He keeps a paid informer in the household of every noble, reads betrayal into ordinary absences, and cannot sit down to dinner without seeing an accusation in an empty chair. Analysts writing about personalist regimes in 2026 describe the same trap: the ruler who purges rivals in order to feel safe manufactures exactly the enemies he feared.\n\nThe play is equally sharp about the institutions that let tyranny stand. Scotland’s nobles see what has happened and calculate. Some flee, some wait, and several spend the middle acts hedging until the outcome looks settled. Only when Macduff and Malcolm raise an army abroad does the country move, and by then whole families have been erased. Shakespeare shows a slow and recognizable process rather than a single shocking coup: a sequence of ordinary compromises made by people telling themselves the situation may yet improve.\n\nThe ending restores order, though warily. Malcolm tests Macduff by pretending to be a worse tyrant than Macbeth, as if the kingdom has learned that a claimant’s promises must be examined before anyone hands him a crown. That instinct, suspicion of the charismatic savior, is the play’s most useful gift to a reader watching modern politics. Legitimate power in Macbeth is not the power that wins the fight; it is the power willing to be questioned.',
