          'The play moves at astonishing speed, and the speed is the argument rather than a quirk of plotting. Romeo enters sighing over Rosaline, a woman he never speaks to on stage, and forgets her completely within an hour of seeing Juliet. The lovers meet, share a sonnet, and kiss inside fourteen lines, marry the following afternoon, and are buried within the week. Friar Laurence, who consents to every stage of it, is also the man who warns that they stumble who run fast.\n\nShakespeare surrounds the couple with adults who move just as recklessly. Capulet tells Paris early on to wait two summers because his daughter is too young, then reverses himself within days, schedules the wedding for the morning after next, and threatens to drag her to church on a hurdle. Tybalt draws steel because he dislikes a stranger’s voice at a party. Romeo buys poison within an hour of hearing a rumor, without asking the servant who brought it a single question. Haste in Verona is not a failing of youth; it is the temperament of the whole city.\n\nThe lovers themselves diagnose the danger accurately and then ignore the diagnosis. At the height of the orchard scene, Juliet calls their contract too rash, too unadvised, too sudden, too like the lightning that stops existing before anyone can name it. That is the play’s method throughout: characters see clearly and act otherwise. What destroys Romeo and Juliet is not the size of their love but the fact that nobody in Verona, including the two of them, will let anything take the time it needs.',
