          'The true antagonist never appears as a character. It is the ancient grudge itself, a hatred so old that Shakespeare never bothers to explain where it came from, because nobody in Verona remembers or cares. The opening brawl starts with servants trading insults and biting thumbs at each other, which tells the audience everything it needs: the quarrel has spread so far beyond its owners that men with nothing to gain will bleed over names they did not choose.\n\nThe feud kills chiefly by making ordinary responses impossible. Romeo cannot court Juliet openly, so the wedding must be secret. He cannot explain to Tybalt why he refuses the challenge, so Mercutio steps forward and dies for a silence Romeo is not free to break. Juliet cannot tell her father she is already married, so she drinks a drug and lies down in a tomb. Every fatal decision in the play is a workaround for a wall that the parents built and the children inherited.\n\nMercutio, the only major figure who belongs to neither house, sees it most clearly, and his dying curse on both families is the play’s verdict rather than a flourish. The reconciliation costs precisely what the Prologue promised: two fathers clasping hands over two corpses and pledging statues of gold, the most useless memorial imaginable. Peace was available on those terms at any moment. It simply required someone to want it before the funerals rather than after.',
