          'Austen’s wit is structural rather than decorative. The opening sentence about a single man in possession of a good fortune arrives dressed as a universal law and turns out to be a report on the opinions of a neighborhood, a joke the second paragraph completes by letting Mrs. Bennet act it out. Any reader who takes the line at face value has already been caught. Within three sentences Austen has trained her audience to listen for the distance between what is said and what is meant.\n\nHer greatest technical achievement is the way the narration slides into Elizabeth’s head without announcement. Sentences that appear to state facts turn out to be reporting Elizabeth’s opinions, so the reader adopts her certainties about Darcy and Wickham as though they were the novel’s own. When the letter arrives, the correction lands on the reader exactly as hard as it lands on the heroine, because we have been convicted of the same prejudice. Few novels implicate their audience so gracefully or so thoroughly.\n\nThe technique also lets Austen be merciless without ever raising her voice. Mr. Collins condemns himself in his own sentences, Lady Catherine believes she is being gracious while behaving monstrously, and Mr. Bennet earns his laughs and then, quietly, his reprimand, since the wit he uses to escape his family is also what leaves Lydia unsupervised. Irony in Austen is not a way of ducking moral judgment. It is the instrument she judges with.',
