        heading: 'First Impressions in a Swipe-Right Culture',
        body:
          'Austen originally called the novel *First Impressions*, and the working title describes the plot better than the one she published. Everything that goes wrong follows from verdicts reached in the first minutes of an acquaintance. Elizabeth settles her opinion of Darcy at a dance and her opinion of Wickham on the strength of a pleasant manner and a good story, and she needs the rest of the book to unpick both. A reader in 2026 who has ever formed a firm view of a stranger from a profile and three photographs is reading about themselves.\n\nThe novel’s sharper insight is that a first impression is not merely inaccurate; it defends itself. Once Elizabeth has decided Darcy is proud, every later observation files itself as further proof, and Wickham’s charm protects him from questions that would collapse his story in a minute. Psychologists now call this confirmation bias. Austen simply dramatizes it, and she takes care to show that Elizabeth is intelligent, observant, and completely wrong, which means cleverness offers no protection whatsoever.\n\nWhat the book recommends is slower and less comfortable than a better first impression. Elizabeth changes her mind only because she is handed detailed evidence in writing and forces herself to read it fairly, twice, in private, against her own interest. That is an unglamorous process and the exact opposite of the instant sorting modern platforms are designed to reward. The quiet claim of the novel is that people become legible only over time, and that any system built for speed will keep manufacturing the mistake Elizabeth spends the whole story correcting.',
