        heading: '"Authenticity" vs. Performance Online',
        body:
          'Holden wages war on performance decades before anyone had a feed to perform on. He notices that people adopt special voices for company, that his brother D.B. traded serious writing for Hollywood money, that the pianist at a fashionable club plays to be admired rather than heard, and that the crowd in an expensive bar is mostly there to be seen. Strip away the 1950s furniture and he is describing the exact instinct that governs a social platform: the constant, low-level adjustment of the self for an audience.\n\nThe novel then complicates the position, which is why it outlasts the sentiment. Holden is himself a tireless performer. He gives a false name to a stranger on the train and spins an elaborate flattering story about her son, lies about his age to order drinks, and puts on the red hunting hat like a costume whenever he needs to feel like somebody. He detests in others precisely what he does all day, and Salinger makes sure the reader notices. Purity about authenticity turns out to be its own kind of pose.\n\nThat is the most useful thing the book offers a reader in 2026 who is tired of curated selves. Holden’s standard is unlivable, because everyone performs, and a demand for total sincerity ends where his three days end: alone, broke, and soaked on a bench. The one moment of genuine peace arrives when he stops appraising anybody and simply watches his sister go around and around on a carousel in the rain. He is no longer grading the performance. He is finally just present for it.',
